ne><line class=3D"up-icon__stroke" x1=3D"24.5" x2=3D"39.5" y1=3D"32" y2=3D"=
32"></line></g></svg></button>

<div class=3D"up-accordion__inner">
<p>In the context of health and wellbeing, self-determination is the right =
of individuals or groups to make their own choices about their healthcare a=
nd to have control over their health destiny. For M=C4=81ori, this means ha=
ving the authority and autonomy to manage their health services, make decis=
ions that affect their health, and maintain their cultural practices within=
 healthcare settings.</p>
</div>
</div>

<h3>What are the Principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi?</h3>

<p>The interpretation of Te Tiriti o Waitangi&nbsp; has long been debated, =
especially in terms of how it should be applied in modern-day Aotearoa New =
Zealand. As we have seen, the two versions (one in M=C4=81ori and one in En=
glish) have differences in language and meaning. To address these differenc=
es, a set of guiding principles were developed to provide clarity and assis=
t in applying the Treaty to government policies, particularly in sectors li=
ke health and education. Apply the principles helps to ensure that the righ=
ts and obligations of both M=C4=81ori and the Crown are upheld.</p>

<h4>Watch - M=C4=81takitaki: Treaty of Waitangi: The Principles (1:32)</h4>

<p>Watch this short video that gives an overview of the development of trea=
ty principles.</p>

<h5>Pre Watch Question</h5>

<p>As you watch, listen out for the answers to this question:</p>

<ul><li>What are the two key principles mentioned in the video?</li>
</ul><div class=3D"up-iframe myvideocontainer"><iframe allow=3D"acceleromet=
er; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen=3D"" al=
lowsinlinemediaplayback=3D"" class=3D"up-iframe__inner" frameborder=3D"0" h=
eight=3D"360" src=3D"cid:frame-EC55E16C91315A098927989032758CCC@mhtml.blink=
" width=3D"640"></iframe></div>

<p>Initially, three overarching principles of Partnership, Protection, and =
Participation (often called the =E2=80=9CThree Ps=E2=80=9D), were establish=
ed by the Royal Commission on Social Policy in the 1980s. These principles =
have since been expanded and adapted to align with present day needs and en=
sure that M=C4=81ori rights under Te Tiriti are actively protected and impl=
emented.</p>

<h4>The Ministry of Health and the Te Tiriti o Waitangi Principles</h4>

<p>As part of their commitment to applying Te Tiriti o Waitangi to the heal=
th and wellbeing sector, the Ministry of Health has implemented five Te Tir=
iti principles for the health system. These five principles were recommende=
d in The Hauora report from the Waitangi Tribunal. They are:</p>

<ul><li><strong>Tino Rangatiratanga- Self-Determination: </strong>This prin=
ciple highlights self-determination and autonomy for M=C4=81ori over their =
health and wellbeing. It supports the empowerment of M=C4=81ori to manage t=
heir own health priorities and outcomes and extends to the planning, implem=
entation, and oversight of health and disability services.</li>
	<li><strong>Equity - Mana Taurite: </strong>The framework promotes equitab=
le healthcare access and outcomes for M=C4=81ori, aiming to address health =
disparities and ensure that M=C4=81ori receive the same level of health and=
 wellbeing as all other New Zealanders.</li>
	<li><strong>Active Protection - Whakamarumarutia: </strong>This requires t=
he health system to actively protect M=C4=81ori health, ensuring that M=C4=
=81ori have access to culturally appropriate services and that their health=
 is prioritised.</li>
	<li><strong>Options - K=C5=8Dwhiringa:</strong> The principle of options e=
nsures that M=C4=81ori have access to both mainstream health services and t=
hose that reflect their cultural values and practices, such as rongo=C4=81 =
M=C4=81ori (traditional M=C4=81ori healing).</li>
	<li><strong>Partnership - P=C4=81tuitanga:</strong> Healthcare organisatio=
ns are expected to work in partnership with M=C4=81ori at all levels of dec=
ision-making and governance, reflecting the Crown=E2=80=99s obligations und=
er Te Tiriti to work alongside tangata whenua in planning and delivering he=
alth services.</li>
</ul><p>Click <a href=3D"https://www.health.govt.nz/maori-health/te-tiriti-=
o-waitangi-framework" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">HERE</a=
> to read about the Te Tiriti o Waitangi framework. Once you have read abou=
t the goals and principles, click on the =E2=80=98Te Tiriti o Waitangi fram=
ework=E2=80=99 link to see an infographic and explanations about the framew=
ork.</p>

<h3>Problems with Principles</h3>

<p>The Treaty principles, such as those used by the Ministry of Health, hav=
e helped bridge gaps between the M=C4=81ori and English versions of the Tre=
aty. However, there is ongoing discussion about their ability to fully hono=
ur the words and original intent of the Treaty. Some argue that the princip=
les, while useful, can be vague and may not adequately address M=C4=81ori s=
overeignty and self-determination (Tino Rangatiratanga).</p>

<p>As a support worker, it is important that you are aware of these discuss=
ions and consider the differing viewpoints when working alongside t=C4=81ng=
ata whaiora (the people you provide care and support for). Understanding th=
ese discussions can help you provide support that respects the cultural val=
ues and rights of M=C4=81ori.</p>
</div>
